# record.schema — which layer may decide each key of the `record` subsystem.
#
# Read by /usr/lib/w/w-conf-lib.sh (and its Python twin) during the load
# pass; a user-layer assignment to a `system` key is dropped with a warning, and
# `wconf_set` refuses to write it in the first place. See w-conf.md.
#
# Format: <KEY>\t<scope>       scope: system | user | both
#   system — root domain: the admin (or fleet policy) decides, per machine.
#   user   — the logged-in user may override it in ~/.config/w/record.conf.
#   both   — either layer may speak; the usual priority order applies.
#
# Every key here is `user`, on the same reasoning as screenshot.schema: a
# recording is one person's capture of their own screen inside their own
# graphical session, and none of these keys reaches past it. A fleet that wants
# to pin a codec or forbid the audio track still can — policy.d sits above the
# user layer.
#
# RECORDER is the one that looks like it should be `system`, since which backends
# exist IS a machine-level fact. It is not: the backend a user may choose is
# bounded by what is installed, which the filesystem already enforces, and
# w-record turns an unavailable backend into an error rather than trusting the
# key. Scope would add a second gate over the same fact.

RECORDER	user
ENCODER	user
AUDIO	user
FORMAT	user
FRAMERATE	user
WINDOW_MASK	user
